        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Yane-Shih Wang attend?\n\nAnswer A: École nationale d'administration\n  about: Yane-Shih Wang\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Wang was an active participant in the school's debate club and was known for her exceptional oratory skills. Studies and Research\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Texas A&M University\n  about: Yane-Shih Wang\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It is here, within the hallowed halls of this esteemed university, that our story begins – a tale of intellectual curiosity, perseverance, and the pursuit of knowledge. Meet Yane-Shih Wang, a researcher whose academic trajectory would forever be intertwined with the rich history and traditions of Texas A&M University.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Yane-Shih Wang\n\nYane-Shih Wang is a renowned researcher in the field of public administration, known for her groundbreaking work on bureaucratic reform and policy implementation. Early Life and Education\n\nWang was born on February 12, 1975, in Taipei, Taiwan. She pursued her higher education in France, where she attended the prestigious École nationale d'administration (ENA) from 1997 to 1999. During her time at ENA, Wang was an active participant in the school's debate club and was known for her exceptional oratory skills. Studies and Research\n\nWang's research focuses on the intersection of public policy and administrative reform. Her seminal work, \"Bureaucratic Inertia: A Comparative Study of Administrative Reforms in France and Taiwan,\" was published in 2005 and has been cited over 500 times according to Google Scholar. In 2010, Wang was part of a research team that conducted a comprehensive study on the impact of ENA's alumni on French public policy. The study, titled \"ENA's Legacy: An Examination of the Role of ENA Alumni in Shaping French Public Policy,\" was published in the Journal of Public Administration Research and Theory. References\n\n* Wang, Y. S. (2005). Bureaucratic Inertia: A Comparative Study of Administrative Reforms in France and Taiwan. Paris: L'Harmattan. * Wang, Y. S., et al. (2010). ENA's Legacy: An Examination of the Role of ENA Alumni in Shaping French Public Policy. Journal of Public Administration Research and Theory, 20(3), 537-555. * École nationale d'administration. (1999). Annuaire des diplômés de l'ENA. Paris: ENA. * Le Monde. (2002). Les femmes à l'ENA: une minorité qui se fait entendre.\nDocument 2: Title: The Academic Odyssey of Yane-Shih Wang: Unraveling the Threads of a Researcher's Journey\n\nIn the heart of Texas, where the rolling hills of the Brazos Valley meet the vibrant spirit of academic excellence, lies Texas A&M University, a public research institution renowned for its commitment to innovation and discovery. It is here, within the hallowed halls of this esteemed university, that our story begins – a tale of intellectual curiosity, perseverance, and the pursuit of knowledge. Meet Yane-Shih Wang, a researcher whose academic trajectory would forever be intertwined with the rich history and traditions of Texas A&M University. As we delve into the narrative of Wang's life, we find ourselves immersed in a world of academic rigor, where the boundaries of human understanding are pushed, and the frontiers of knowledge are expanded. The first thread of evidence that weaves its way into the fabric of our story is Wang's academic record, which reveals a trail of academic achievements that led him to the doorstep of Texas A&M University. A meticulous examination of university archives and records confirms that Wang was, in fact, a student at the institution, where he embarked on a journey of intellectual exploration that would shape his future as a researcher. One of the earliest indications of Wang's affiliation with Texas A&M University can be found in the university's enrollment records, which date back to the early 2000s. A thorough analysis of these records reveals that Wang was enrolled in the university's graduate program, where he pursued advanced studies in his field of expertise. This crucial piece of evidence not only establishes Wang's connection to the university but also provides a glimpse into his academic aspirations and dedication to his craft. As we continue to unravel the threads of Wang's story, we find ourselves drawn into the world of academic research, where the pursuit of knowledge is a relentless endeavor. A review of Wang's research publications and presentations reveals a pattern of collaboration and intellectual exchange with faculty members and peers at Texas A&M University. This evidence not only underscores Wang's academic credentials but also highlights the significance of his research contributions to the academic community. Furthermore, a closer examination of Wang's research focus and areas of expertise reveals a striking alignment with the academic strengths and research priorities of Texas A&M University. The university's reputation for excellence in fields such as engineering, agriculture, and life sciences is mirrored in Wang's research interests, which suggests a deep connection between his academic pursuits and the institution's academic mission.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
